1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,000
What if a giant planet came drifting into our solar system, and passed right by Earth?

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,780
Not a comet. Not an asteroid. A whole planet, as big as Jupiter, with no sun of its own.

3
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:15,100
These rogue planets are real. Our galaxy may hold more of them than stars.

4
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:21,030
Year one. Astronomers spot it out past Neptune, racing toward us at twenty-six kilometres every second.

5
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:26,430
They check the numbers again and again. It won't hit us. It will miss Earth by ten million kilometres.

6
00:00:26,430 --> 00:00:32,790
Sounds safe? That's only twenty-six times farther than the Moon. And this thing weighs as much as three hundred Earths.

7
00:00:32,790 --> 00:00:39,100
Year five. It's the brightest thing in the night sky after the Moon. With binoculars, you can see its stripes.

8
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:45,550
The week of the pass, it grows bigger than the full Moon. Nobody sleeps. Everyone is outside, staring up.

9
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:51,750
But its gravity tugs on Earth even harder than the Moon does. Tides surge, and seaside towns are told to leave.

10
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:56,930
Scientists even watch for earthquakes, because strong tides can tip faults that are ready to slip.

11
00:00:56,930 --> 00:01:01,260
Then, in just a few days, it's gone. But it takes something with it.

12
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:04,600
Its pull drags Earth's whole orbit, by up to three percent.

13
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:08,600
A little closer to the Sun, and Earth could get hotter than any climate forecast.

14
00:01:08,600 --> 00:01:14,190
A little farther, and we could slide into an ice age. Even the year could change by two weeks.

15
00:01:14,190 --> 00:01:19,100
But the strangest part comes as it leaves. Telescopes zoom in on one of its moons.

16
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,820
And on its night side, there are lights.

17
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:29,170
Cities. A civilization that has drifted through the dark for millions of years. Our Sun is the first star they've ever seen up close.

18
00:01:29,170 --> 00:01:32,970
Then, a message reaches every phone on Earth, at the same time.

19
00:01:32,970 --> 00:01:36,920
Your star is warm. Please. Let us stay.

20
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:45,830
Their planet leaves in three days, back into the dark forever. They could send millions of their people here. Nobody knows if they're refugees, or invaders.

21
00:01:45,830 --> 00:01:48,080
Mum? Are they scared too?

22
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,500
Three days.

23
00:01:49,530 --> 00:01:50,500
Two.

24
00:01:50,550 --> 00:01:51,530
One.

25
00:01:51,620 --> 00:01:54,460
Would you let them stay? What would you choose?

